  "id": "Compound::DB00496 - Gene::1128",
  "verbalization": "Darifenacin, a cholinergic muscarinic antagonist, is implicated in its relationship with Chrm1 through multiple interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Darifenacin binds to Chrm3, which is part of the G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity, and this activity is linked to Chrm1. This implies a potential mechanism where Darifenacin modulates the activity of Chrm1 through its interaction with Chrm3, possibly influencing downstream signaling pathways. Additionally, Darifenacin is included in the class of cholinergic muscarinic antagonists, which includes Flavoxate and Solifenacin, both of which are known to bind to Chrm1. This suggests a broader pharmacological context where Darifenacin may exert its effects through a common mechanism involving muscarinic receptors.\n\nThe evidence is further supported by the presence of a lowest common ancestor (LCA) between Darifenacin and Flavoxate, as well as between Darifenacin and Solifenacin, both of which share the same class of compounds. This reinforces the idea that Darifenacin and its analogs are part of a pharmacological group that interacts with Chrm1. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism where Darifenacin modulates Chrm1 through interactions with muscarinic receptors, possibly by inhibiting their activity or altering their signaling. The evidence supports a direct or indirect relationship between Darifenacin and Chrm1, mediated through the muscarinic receptor pathway.",
